You've probably heard of the term depth maps thrown around before, but never really understood what they actually do. So, in this video, I'm going to show you exactly what they are, what they can be used for, and how you can start using them in your own edits. What are depth maps? At first glance, a depth map just looks like a random black and white image, but it's actually telling the software how far everything in your scene is from the camera. Unlike a normal image that stores color information, a depth map stores distance. White represents the parts closest to the camera, black represents the parts farthest away, and gray is everything in between. Normally, [music] the software just sees your footage as one flat image and has no idea what's in front or behind anything. A depth map gives it that missing depth information, letting effects react to the scene much more naturally. [music] What can they do? Once the software knows what's close and what's far away, you can do some really cool things with it. Probably the most common use is depth of field, where you can blur just the background while

keeping your subject perfectly sharp. You can also create atmospheric effects like fog, smoke, rain, and light rays that naturally wrap around the object instead of just sitting on top of the entire image. At the end of the day, depth maps are just another compositing tool that let your effects understand the depth of your scene instead of treating everything like one flat image.

How do you use them? Using a depth map is actually pretty simple. Most effects that support them will have an option where you can select a depth map as an input. Once you assign it, the effects use that grayscale image to figure out what's closest to the camera and what's farther away. From there, it's really just a matter of adjusting settings depending on the effect that you're using. For something like camera lens blur, just set your depth map as the blur map, then tweak the settings until you get the look you're after. For Z fog linear, set the Z buffer to your depth map, pick whatever fog color you want, then adjust the settings until it blends naturally into your scene. You can also use depth maps to dissolve parts of your scene based on depth. Here, I'm using gradient wipe with my depth map so the background can gradually fade away while the foreground stays visible. From there, just adjust the completion and softness until you get the result you want. And if you've seen my previous video, you can even use them for realistic 3D lighting. I made an entire

